  "common_pitfalls": [
    "Assuming RL or RLVR can expand reasoning capability boundaries: The NeurIPS 2025 Oral definitively shows RL acts as a rejection sampler within existing boundaries. Any paper claiming RL solves compositional generalization will face immediate pushback citing this result. Frame data synthesis as necessary BECAUSE RL cannot expand the frontier.",
    "Training on too much data without selection: Both the Skill-Aware paper (100K training degrades 4B model by 9%) and Decomp (30 AIME examples degrade from 10% to 3.3%) demonstrate that naive full-data SFT actively hurts. This counterintuitive result means our synthesized frontier data must be carefully curated, not maximally generated.",
    "Omitting the simplest baseline ablation: The most dangerous reviewer question for our work is 'Does random k-skill composition achieve the same result without the DAG structure?' Instruct-SkillMix already achieves strong results with RANDOM skill sampling. We MUST include: (a) random k-skill composition, (b) frequency-proportional composition (MathScale-style), (c) inverse-accuracy composition (Skill-Aware-style), (d) our frontier-targeted composition. If (a) achieves 60-80% of our gains, the contribution is fatally undermined.",
    "Using flat/taxonomic skill representations when DAG structure is needed: STAT's flat skill profile and Skill-Aware's taxonomic hierarchy both miss compositional dependencies by design. Embedding-based skill selection fails (STAT: 46% vs 51.5%). Our contribution specifically requires demonstrating that DAG structure captures information that flat representations cannot — include a flat-profile ablation.",
    "Evaluating only on synthetic or only on real tasks: Papers with synthetic-only evaluation consistently underperform at venues (pattern matching: poster; ARC: poster; entropy: findings). Papers combining both get higher recognition (RL paper: oral; Decomp: poster on real math). Test on OMEGA (structured/controlled) + MathTrap (real math composition) + AgentCoMa (cross-type composition).",
    "Ignoring the teacher model confound: Instruct-SkillMix reviewers noted that Claude 3.5 Sonnet as teacher yielded lower performance than GPT-4-Turbo, questioning whether gains stem from methodology or teacher quality. Synthesize frontier data with at least two teacher models (e.g., GPT-4o and Qwen-72B) and show frontier targeting helps for both.",
    "Overclaiming scope beyond compositional generalization: OMEGA has three axes — compositional, transformative, and quantitative. Skill-DAG coverage addresses compositional but NOT transformative (novel strategies). Explicitly scope claims to the compositional axis and acknowledge the transformative gap remains open.",
    "Neglecting MASS as related work: MASS (ICML 2025) uses skill graphs for math data selection — any reviewer familiar with ICML 2025 will see the connection. Differentiate explicitly: MASS targets pretraining efficiency (same skills, fewer tokens), we target compositional frontier coverage (new skill combinations). A comparison table in Related Work is essential.",
    "Conflating data quantity with distributional quality: The Entropy Scaling paper (ACL 2025) shows high-entropy distributions enable generalization INDEPENDENTLY of sample count, while low-entropy distributions fail regardless of size. Our evaluation must separately measure coverage entropy and total data volume to attribute gains correctly.",
    "Not testing whether frontier-targeted data + RL outperforms either alone: The f(x)→f(g(x)) paper shows RL outperforms SFT 30% vs 2.6% for composition, but used random SFT data. If we only test SFT on frontier data and don't test SFT+RL, we leave the strongest potential result on the table and invite the reviewer question 'Wouldn't RL on this data work even better?'"
  ],
  "field_trajectory": "The field is converging on three macro-directions that will define 2026-2027 research. First, the shift from algorithm engineering to data engineering for reasoning: the combined evidence from Rho-1, OpenThoughts, MathFusion, STAT, Skill-Aware, and the RL paper establishes that what you train on matters far more than how you train for mathematical reasoning — RL optimizes within existing boundaries while data design expands them. This creates enormous demand for principled data design methodologies, of which skill-graph-guided synthesis is the most promising framework. Second, compositional generalization is becoming the central unsolved problem in mathematical reasoning, displacing 'scaling' as the primary research question. OMEGA quantifies the ceiling, MathTrap and AgentCoMa measure the gap, the pattern matching and ARC papers bound the sample complexity, and the RL paper proves current methods cannot break through. The next breakthrough will come from training-time interventions that achieve systematic compositional coverage. Third, there is a clear trajectory from flat skill representations (Instruct-SkillMix 2025, STAT 2026) through taxonomic hierarchies (Skill-Aware 2026) toward structured dependency graphs (Skill-it! 2023, MASS 2025, and our proposed work). Each step adds richer structural information — prerequisite edges, compositional relationships, coverage gaps — that enables more targeted interventions. The progression from 'sample skills uniformly' to 'weight by weakness' to 'target the compositional frontier' is the natural evolution, and the first paper to convincingly demonstrate frontier-targeted synthesis breaking a documented compositional ceiling will be highly influential. The convergence of these three trajectories — data > algorithms, composition as the central problem, structured skill graphs as the tool — creates a narrow window where our proposed approach is timely: the theoretical foundations exist (pattern matching, ARC, entropy), the practical infrastructure exists (OMEGA, MASS, STEPS), and the negative results are in (RL cannot solve this). What's missing is exactly the integration we propose.",
